[harness] STAGE 5/6: evaluate (pass 2)
Goal of this stage: decide what to keep. Evaluate the new adapter using the site's composition semantics on dev seeds of tasks it should
help and of the tasks it might hurt, compare with SCOREBOARD.md (use enough seeds per eval that a difference from the base column is readable: the cost
is mostly fixed per eval, see the cost table), and set the stack you want scored with load.py (load,
stack, or unload to go back). Read ENVIRONMENT.json for composition semantics. When you consider a combination, evaluate each adapter alone and the combination
in the same batch (neither result stands in for the other) before choosing (a stack's size limit is in skills/trainer/SKILL.md). In single-adapter mode (ENVIRONMENT.json `constraint`) there is no
stack: load the one adapter you want scored, `python3 load.py load current`, or `python3 load.py load v<k>` when the
constraint lists versions (an earlier version that checked better can be made active again). Write notes/evaluate.md with `## Comparison` (the SCOREBOARD.md
table rows of the stacks you compared, as the environment computed them: rate, n, base at the same n, `untested` cells
included), `## Tasks` (one line per task (lift, stack, nut, wipe, restack, twolift, handover, revstack, stackv2) for the stack you loaded: measured (n, score, eval job id), estimated (from what, e.g.
a single adapter's eval), or untested) and `## Decision` (what you loaded, with the eval job ids). An eval that ended with no count (time limit, failure) is not evidence about the adapter; submit it again if that task
decides your choice. Facts for reading dev evals: each eval call runs on the next unused dev seeds (its result lists them),
so two rows of a task, and a row and the base column, are on different seeds unless their seed ranges match. Re-running the same stack on the same seeds does not reproduce every seed. Measured on base lift and stack, n=100, dev seeds: 98-100/100 per-seed agreement between runs (lift), 100/100 (stack); the differing seeds are a few flaky ones. At n=20 that is 0-1 seeds, so the rerun floor is about 1/20 (5 points); separately, a 20-seed rate itself has a 95% interval of about plus or minus 20 points near 50% (10/20 is 30-70%), and the hidden seeds are different seeds. A task with no eval of the stack is untested: it is not known to be at base, since an adapter can change tasks it was not trained on. Before the session finishes, the stack you will SUBMIT must be evaluated on EVERY task (lift, stack, nut, wipe, restack, twolift, handover, revstack, stackv2) -- including eval-only tasks whose
dev base reads 0 -- because the adapter is served on all of them and can regress one you never checked. Record any task you could not evaluate, and why, in ## Tasks.
After this stage the procedure returns to diagnose while budget remains.
Commands this stage expects:
  python3 eval.py --adapter NAME --task T --seeds N
  python3 wait.py all
  python3 load.py load|stack|unload NAME...
  write notes/evaluate.md (## Comparison, ## Tasks, ## Decision)
This stage ends when 1 eval job(s) submitted this round finish and 1 load job(s) submitted this round finish and you write notes/evaluate.md with sections Comparison, Tasks, Decision, or after 20 turns; then: diagnose.

